    "model": "gemini-3.1-pro-preview",
    "generated": "2026-09-11",
    "what_it_is": "Hexyl butyrate is a fatty acid ester that gives off a bright, fruity aroma reminiscent of apples and citrus. Formed naturally when hexanol meets butyric acid, it is widely used as a fragrance and flavor ingredient. You most likely encounter it in scented products, fruit-flavored foods, or simply by taking a deep breath around certain fragrant plants.",
    "found_in": "This fruity ester is found naturally in familiar plants like lavender, apricot, and banana, as well as in hemp (Cannabis sativa). It is also a major component of the essential oil of Persian hogweed (Heracleum persicum).",
    "why_researchers_care": "Researchers are drawn to hexyl butyrate primarily for its powerful ecological role as a volatile organic compound that acts as a signaling molecule and sex attractant for various insects. Beyond its environmental functions, scientists are exploring its potential as a natural antimicrobial agent and insect repellent. The strongest evidence for its biological effects in humans currently comes from observational studies (Oxford level 2b), though much of the foundational work remains in animal and laboratory models.",
